            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Neuron Doctrine</strong><br>\nThe neuron doctrine, developed by Cajal, posited that the nervous system is made up of discrete individual cells. This revolutionary idea contradicted the prevailing belief that the nervous system had a continuous network and was fundamental to the development of modern neuroscience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine</strong><br>\nIn 1906, Cajal was co-awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Camillo Golgi for their work on the structure of the nervous system. This recognition cemented his status as one of the leading scientists of his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cajal's Scientific Methods</strong><br>\nCajal was renowned for his meticulous techniques and the precision of his drawings of neurons. His artistic ability translated into scientific illustration, providing clear and accurate representations of his observations which were essential for his analyses.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
